speechtext:
FIELDING (Minister of Finance) moved that the House go into committee to consider the following proposed resolution;
That it is expedient to approve of the Supplementary Convention respecting the commercial relations between Canada and France, entered into at Paris on the 23rd day of January, 1909, by plenipotentiaries appointed by His Majesty and by the President of the French Republic, a copy of which was on the eighth day of March, 1909, transmitted to the House of Commons by His Excellency the Governor General.
He said:	With your permission, Mr. Speaker, I would suggest that it would probably be more convenient for the House to discuss this subject, should discussion be found necessary, when the Bill is printed. We might pass the resolution through committee as the foundation for the Bill that is to be introduced, but not proceed further with the matter until the Bill has been distributed; in the meantime any information on the subject that is to be given will be brought down.

I am afraid that my hon. friend from Huron (Mr. Chisholm) must have been misled bv somebody when this matter was before parliament at an earlier stage, because he certainly has an erroneous conception. He did not get it from this side of the House, and if he was led into error he must blame some one else. Let us understand what is the point of difference between my hon. friend and myself. We must connect his present observations with those that occurred a few minutes ago. He stated that by this .treaty we were introducing brandy and absinthe at reduced rates. That is the point of difference between my hon. friend and myself, that is the basis of his whole speech.
7}
He is wrong. This treaty does not provide for the introduction of brandy or absinthe or any one of these things at a reduced rate. When I tell my hon. friend that, the whole foundation of his case is gone.
